<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1950 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sodade</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Cape Verdean song of longing for people who have left the islands, made famous by the singer Cesária Évora in 1992.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Armando Zeferino Soares</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Armando Zeferino Soares</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Cape Verdeans and the Cape Verdean emigrant diaspora</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cape Verdean Creole</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cape Verde</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1950s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music credited to Armando Zeferino Soares)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Sodade&quot; is a Cape Verdean song written in the 1950s by Armando Zeferino Soares. Its name is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verdean-creole">Cape Verdean Creole</a> form of the Portuguese word saudade, a longing for what is absent. Its authorship was contested, notably by the duo Amândio Cabral and Luís Morais, until a court ruled in December 2006 that Soares was the author.</p><p>The song belongs to the long history of Cape Verdean emigration. Cape Verdeans have been seafarers and migrants for centuries and have gone of their own accord to every continent since the early 1800s, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, where they were recruited as whalers and whaling captains, and later in times of drought and after independence from Portugal. &quot;Sodade&quot; refers to a different movement: the departure of a small part of the population as contract labourers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar">António de Oliveira Salazar</a>'s authoritarian rule over Portugal and its colonies. Departures, called despididas, were long marked with mornas sung as a farewell serenade to family and neighbours, and &quot;Sodade&quot; stands in that tradition; the history of migration is why morna is so often melancholy.</p><p>Bonga recorded &quot;Sodade&quot; on his album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> 74 in 1974, and Cesária <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> made it widely known on her 1992 album Miss Perfumado. Kepa Junkera and Dulce Pontes recorded it in 1998. Madonna sang it on her Madame X Tour of 2019 and 2020, with the Cape Verdean singer Dino d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> dates, again in Harlem in 2021, and in Portugal on her Celebration Tour in November 2023.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A morna whose theme is the longing of those left behind when Cape Verdeans emigrate; its mood is melancholy and nostalgic.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="130289" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="8118186" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Cesaria-evora-sodade-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-cesaria-evora-sodade-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="ku_WZoTtT8Q" data-title="Sodade: Cesária Évora, the official video"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Cesária Évora, the official video</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel CesariaEvoraVEVO. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ku_WZoTtT8Q" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Angola 74 (Bonga)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Miss Perfumado (Cesária Évora)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1992</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bilbao 00:00 h (Kepa Junkera with Dulce Pontes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Madame X Tour (Madonna)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">live performance, with Dino d'Santiago in Lisbon</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Madame X: Music from the Theatre Xperience (Madonna with Dino d'Santiago)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2023</span><span class="sg-chain-h">bonus track on the vinyl soundtrack</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Morna farewell songs (despididas)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century onwards</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the song stands in this tradition</span></li></ul></section>
